WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.630
Looking to get high on a new water sport.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.740
Well, you literally can riding a flying
water bike.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:12.830
It attaches with a long hose to the back of
a jet ski, elevating you up to almost 40ft

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.580
above the water where you can perform aerial
flips and spins.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:21.890
The jet skis, water thrust feeds three jet
nozzles on the flying water bike.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.830
One propels the bike upward, the other two
feed jet control nozzles that maneuver the

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.560
bike. The bike's frame is made out of
aluminum parts because it has to be

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:38.540
lightweight as well as relatively corrosion
resistant, especially if it's going to be

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:39.920
used in salt water.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:46.910
This curved tube will become the lifting jet
to build each jet control nozzle.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:52.190
A worker places a housing on an alignment
fixture, then hammers an end cap into

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.520
position, holding the housing steady with a
clamp.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.190
The worker welds on the end cap.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.480
This housing will cover the bushing at the
heart of the jet control nozzle.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.020
He inserts an elbow into the opening in the
end cap and welds the parts together.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:17.290
After welding a jet nozzle cone to the other
end of the elbow, he welds a handlebar to the

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:23.350
housing. These five welded parts make up the
body of the first jet control nozzle.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.920
To prep for painting the manufacturers rough
up all the aluminum parts by tumbling them

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.480
with abrasive stones.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:38.120
This component splits the portion of the
water thrust that bypasses the lifting jet,

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.960
sending it in opposite directions to the jet
control nozzles.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:47.500
The lift and jet tube now has a nozzle cone
welded to one end.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:53.140
A coupling for attaching the fire hose to the
other and an oval hole in the curve which

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:57.310
aligns with the angled end of a straight
tube in the middle called the backbone.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:08.090
After positioning parallel support bars
between the lifting jet and the splitter, the

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.430
welder fuses all the parts together.

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:16.400
Then he flips the assembly jig and welds on
the seat support.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.590
The seat support attaches where the lifting
jet and backbone meet.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.890
After masking the ends of the splitter, a
worker sprays the completed frame, jet

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.280
control nozzles and other aluminum parts
with powder coat.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:37.010
This helps protect against corrosion.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.450
The parts go into an oven for up to a half
hour to bake.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.670
The powder coat to a shiny finish.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:51.270
Then workers install an aluminum cam lock to
securely fasten the fire hose to the

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:56.700
coupling. Another worker attaches the
remaining components to the frame.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.870
First, he screws in the pair of foot plates
on which the rider stands.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.210
Then he bolts the bottom half of each plate
to the parallel support bars and the top half

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.070
to just ahead of the cam lock.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.310
He mounts a padded knee rest to the support
bars.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.230
It can be easily repositioned to accommodate
the rider's height.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:28.080
On each unpainted end of the splitter, he
installs an internal support ring, then a

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.680
precision fit nylon bushing and an external
support ring which he screws to the splitter.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:45.550
The worker slides the jet control nozzle
housing over the bushing and screws it to the

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:46.870
internal support ring.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.370
The two control nozzles maneuver the water
bike to ascend.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:56.050
You pull back gradually on both of them
simultaneously to descend.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.970
You push forward on both to spin.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:03.580
You move one forward and one back and to do
a backflip.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.310
You yank both control nozzles all the way
back.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.900
He assembles the water bike's body.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:16.090
It's made of molded polyethylene with a
waterproof urethane pad to cushion the front

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.350
of the rider's body.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:22.810
The seat is made of the same padded
material, both pad and seat attach with

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.040
plastic clips.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:31.600
The hollow body is completely watertight,
which is why the bike floats.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.190
The final step is to mate the body to the
frame.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.900
The completed water bicycle weighs £30.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:48.940
Once connected to a jet ski with a fire
hose, this water bike flies at a speed of up

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.620
to 20mph.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:58.330
By maneuvering the jet nozzles, it does
backflips, multiple spins and even dives 20ft

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:05.700
under water. A throttle position sensor
reports the position of the throttle plate to

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:10.800
the engine computer so that it can decide
how much fuel to inject into the engine and

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.120
many other things.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:17.910
Without this critical sensor, the ratio of
the air fuel mix would be compromised and the

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.740
engine would likely stall.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.910
An internal combustion engines.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:30.940
The throttle position sensor is on constant
alert, sensing the necessary power demands to

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.590
ensure a smooth ride.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:36.370
Making these sensors starts with printed
circuit boards.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.930
These boards will ultimately process
physical data and convert this into

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.730
information the engine computer will
understand.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:50.560
A robot transfers the boards to a conveyor,
which takes them to a solder screen printer.

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:56.230
The printer squeegee solder through a
stencil to deposit it in specific locations

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.280
on the boards.

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:04.060
The squeegee and stencil then retract and
the conveyor shoves the boards forward to the

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.320
next operation.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:14.170
Here, a robot retrieves electronic
components from tape on a reel and deposits

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:16.540
them on the solder spots on the boards.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:24.070
It collects multiple parts from the reel at
one time and installs them on the boards

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:25.810
faster than you can blink an eye.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:32.380
Moving forward, the printed circuit boards
arrive at a camera station.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.660
The camera clicks away and sends pictures of
the boards to a computer.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:45.820
The computer analyzes the placement of each
component with the computer's.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.960
Okay. The boards head into an oven with
different heating zones.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:56.440
The oven melts the solder and then cools it
down to fuse the components to the boards.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.420
The oven that ejects the censor boards.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:08.110
Next, a technician serves them up to
automated test probes.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:14.860
The probes energize the components on the
boards and in the process collect

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:20.560
information. They send the data to a
computer which analyzes the function of the

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.250
components on the board and to make sure
they're fully operational.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.440
Then the boards move to a pneumatic punch.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:36.400
It separates the 18 sensor circuit boards by
cutting the tabs that hold them together.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:42.790
A worker assembles a metal spring to a
plastic magnet holder.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:48.010
He places a high wear washer over the neck
of the magnet holder.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:56.050
Then he inserts the magnet holder assembly
into the throttle sensor housing and places

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:57.400
the unit in a tray.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:02.830
After glue has been injected in the holders,
a robot moves in.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:07.970
It scans the holders and confirms that
there's glue in each cavity.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:11.810
It then inserts magnets in the holders.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.270
A worker installs a torsion spring in the
housing.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:21.770
It will hold the magnet holder tight to the
throttle body.

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:31.070
He now inserts the printed circuit boards
into the sensor housings terminals in the

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.500
housings made two slots in the boards.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:41.120
He solders the terminals to the boards to
establish the connections.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.710
All the parts are now in place for throttle
position sensing.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.450
A worker places covers over the sensor board
compartments.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:01.990
An ultrasonic welder, then melts the plastic
at the interfaces.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:07.120
Once it hardens the weld bonds, the cover to
the sensor body.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:13.690
A robot pipes silicone adhesive on the
welded seam to create a double layer of

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:18.830
protection. The sensors ride on a conveyor
that goes through an oven.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:24.910
This cures both the silicone on the welded
seam and the glue inside to secure the magnet

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:28.300
to the holder out of the oven.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.280
The sensors travel through a cooling
station.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:38.950
Probes, then power the devices and calibrate
them for use in specific vehicles.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:46.750
A robot holds the sensor in front of a laser
which etches the date of manufacture back on

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:52.270
a conveyor. The throttle position sensor
travels under a magnifying glass so that the

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:54.850
laser etching job can be scrutinised.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:59.990
This throttle position sensor is now ready
for detection duty.

